## Overview of the project

Digital Project Rewriting the Rules of American Popstardom - Through Kwame Anthony Appiah, “Classification: Talking Identity” Framework
This digital project conducted by Ally. J. Levine & Tiana Mcgee gives a in-depth preview of a new face in mainstream popstardom, Bad Bunny. The article highlights how Bad Bunny embraces his heritage and Latino identity as a central contributor to his breakthrough of success. Rather than conforming to traditional, socially accepted rules and standards within the American pop market, he cultivated norms of how listeners can indulge in the Spanish music world-wide. The article is not just about his musical success within the last decade; it represents his ethnicity and cultural identity as part of his artistic strength. Bad Bunny is viewed through many different lenses by various groups of people. A hero among Latin communities, being a symbol of Spanish pride, A breakthrough superstar who shook up the U.S. music scene that changed the industry, and music in general, showing that English doesn't need to be a standard for global listening. I will be comparing this concept to *The Lies That Bind* framework, a book in week 2 of CTS*3000 by Anothy Appiah, which helps rethink identity, through creed, country, colour, class, and culture.[^1]

## Data activism and identity

While this digital project focuses on data visualizations of Bad Bunny’s career and significance, it highlights a broader cultural shift. The strategic use of “analytics by citizens and civil society to help advance social change”[^2] is the best way this activates a form of data activism through charts and metrics used to reframe narratives about who belongs in major cultural spaces.  

By looking at this article, it instills confidence in Latin culture, the way Bad Bunny is able to expand chart rankings, awards, and global reach of “His songs that blend Latin trap and reggaeton with pop, rock and classic Puerto Rican styles including salsa, bomba and plena”[^3]. He is disrupting norms of Anglo Centric entertainment, where assumptions that global success requires English. Bad Bunny’s label matters, and everyone else believes that to be true globally aswell. In *The Lies That Bind* it discusses how “identities matter to people” [^1] This challenges hegemonies of power through culture, when influence is visualized through numbers it’s hard for the world to dismiss this, it becomes difficult to minimize or stereotype it (like your uncle saying who is this guy when he comes on during the Superbowl).

