## Ethics and affected communities
Bad Bunny’s uprising through his various albums, singles, and eventually leading to the Super Bowl halftime performance. His career trajectory, streaming numbers, and awards are turned into quantified data. The data visualizations can reinforce his global power, which grows his public perception, which is quickly making him a household name. An ethical dynamic that has been circulating across the internet is the political ethical implications. The performance of Bad Bunny speculates theories of how he debates about immigration, race, and national belonging. “While Bad Bunny did not mention ICE at the Super Bowl, the singer has slammed violence linked to immigration in recent days [^4].” By being the face of an affected community, Bad Bunny is looked at as a token of hope through speech, which creates an ethical dilemma in how he should portray himself.  

Communities impacted by the data set could be viewed as everybody. Specifically global streaming audiences tuning in, NFL audiences, Spanish speaking/bilingual audiences, and Puerto Rican communities being symbolically represented. The halftime show translates the impact into metrics like streaming numbers and rankings. “Understanding the culture of quantification that dominates much of our thinking about value and meaning”[^5]. These stats can brainwash what we think is valuable by what is currently popular and shape how people view entertainment.  


## The author's argument and sources

One main argument that the author of this project is trying to present is how Bad Bunny is breaking barriers of how a traditional artist becomes popular worldwide. “Historically, Latin artists have sung in English as a way to increase their audience. Artists like Shakira and Ricky Martin began recording in Spanish and later turned to English,” and 'That’s why people were recording in English, you know, so that they could get into the main sections”[^6] she said. Bad Bunny is showing his identity through his culture, and it has shown to connect with people. He does not align with the norms historically associated with white American mainstream acceptability. “Bad Bunny’s connection to his cultural heritage resonates with fans, even though many come from different backgrounds”. The interface presents data through Spanish statistics, such as the growing spending on Latin music by US consumers, and displays Spanish music awards alongside American ones. The site presents this information with a  Puerto Rican lens, adding cultural items across the project. Pictures like a Pava (a rural Puerto Rican farmer's hat), a Concho  (a Puerto Rican toad), and a Flor de Maga (the official flower of Puerto Rico). Displaying these cultural items works with the site is saying, giving a reader a more in-depth view of a growing culture due to Bad Bunny.  

 
In the article's Super Bowl halftime show piece, the author draws on the Bad Bunny Syllabus as a key source to contextualize the artist’s cultural significance and the historical factors behind his success. The syllabus, a curated educational project that explores Puerto Rican history, reggaetón, colonialism, language politics, and Latinx cultural movements, provides background on the social and political forces that shape Bad Bunny’s music and identity. Other various sources are being used on particular stories being told on his journey. For example using sources for his recent Oscar speech declaring his “ICE Out” and “Were not aliens” speech accepting his award (Broadway & Goller, 2026). The data has been processed in this digital project by different checkpoints in Bad Bunny’s career and how he's made an impact that has been shown to make an impact on various groups of people. ​


