## Design and visualization

The data has been visualized through charts, lists, and types of infographic designs to make visual representations. Combining data and images to create the information of how Bad Bunny’s career has flourished music-wise through his numerous awards and fashion-wise, explaining his popular outfits at the Met Gala, “Bad Bunny has a look That is putting him on the map.” Vogue, 2018. Instead of showing plain tables of numbers, the article explains the data through written storytelling. It adds background about history and culture to help readers understand what the numbers mean, connecting Bad Bunny’s success to bigger changes in representation and the music industry. 

The design elements of the digital project help shape how readers interpret the story. By incorporating visual cues associated with Puerto Rican culture, such as bold colour palettes, stylized typography, and imagery tied to Bad Bunny’s aesthetic, the piece frames his success as culturally grounded rather than culturally neutral. These design choices encourage readers to see the halftime show not just as entertainment, but as an expression of Puerto Rican identity and pride. 

This opens up interpretations that center cultural celebration, visibility, and representation.

While you can't interact with the data in the project, you do get fun and very easy ways to intake the data given. Above through a simplistic chart style of data using blue as an accent colour to a light tan to make it easily visible and understandable. 

